	abstract = {Recently valproate (VPA) has been widely used for the treatment of psychiatric disorders, though it was developed as an anticonvulsant in 1963. VPA is classified as a mood stabilizer with definite antimanic effects and probable antidepressant effects. Several lines of evidence suggest that VPA has a prophylactic effect on bipolar affective disorders, especially rapid cycling ones. It seems to be effective in the treatment of major depression, panic disorder, and behavioral problems associated with dementia, mental retardation, or personality disorder. GABAergic system was once thought to play a major role in the mechanisms of VPA action. Recent studies, however, have paid much attention to the effects of VPA on intracellular signal transduction and neuroprotection to elucidate the mechanisms of its action. An increase in the release of glutamate, a decrease in inositol uptake, an increase in the levels of B cell lymphoma protein-2 (bcl-2), and an inhibition of glycogen synthase kinase-3β (GSK-3β) are probable mechanisms in common with lithium.},
